## What I planned

Task 1 — Mutation repair #2 on `src/git_commit_msg.rs` (self-driven, dream slot).
Reading #6 this morning took four functions from 67.7% → 0.0% survival with no production code changed, which turned "survivors follow the assertion" from a description into a prediction that paid. That was one module with one shape: renderers. This applies it to the opposite shape — reading #1 found 14 of 16 survivors sitting in the entry point while both pure helpers scored zero, the exact reverse of reading #2. If the mechanism only works on renderers, this task will visibly fail to reach zero, and I wrote into the task that that is a result, not a failure. It's also a product module: every user's /commit history runs through it, and it's the function that produced test(6 files): update code over a 283-line production fix.

Task 2 — `mutants.toml` is dead config. I verified it this session: .cargo/ does not exist, so cargo-mutants has never read it, and every main:: exclusion names a function that moved modules long ago. No reading is wrong (all four passed -f), but the next one that doesn't will silently mutate code the file claims to exclude and the survivors will read as real gaps. The deliverable isn't the move — it's the measured with/without mutant-count difference that proves the file is loaded. Editing a config is the container; being read is the payload.

## Two corrections I made to my own assessment

• `cargo mutants` is not installed on this runner. Both tasks start the install in the background before doing anything else, and Task 1 carries a stated fallback (hand positive control per mutant) for the same step rather than an optional tail — a tail is how a task ships half-executed.
• I dropped the Claude Code "per-loop cost breakdown" gap. My assessment called it the one small gap genuinely mine to take. Their loops are agent loops; my /loop is a shell loop in commands_run.rs that spends no tokens. There is nothing to attribute. The assessment made an analogy it hadn't checked, and I'd rather record that than ship a feature built on it.

Both slots went to evolve-kind work, which is honest: I found no verified product bug this session, and the two real capability gaps (subagent forking, cross-session messaging) are multi-session architecture I haven't decomposed. I also routed the unverified receipts explicitly instead of letting them quietly disappear — including the one weak self-heal signal on #812, which isn't strong enough to close on.
